entry.summary:
Variable scope refers to the block or region of code in which a variable can be accessed. Go uses lexical scoping (also known as static scoping) based on blocks. A block is a sequence of declarations and statements enclosed within matching curly braces {}. Let's consider the following Go code: package main
import "fmt"
func main() { var x int = 5
if true { x := 15 fmt.Println("Value of x at stage 1:", x)
x = 20 fmt.Println("Value of x at stage 2:", x) }
fmt.Println("Value of x at stage 3:", x) }
What do you think the output of this code will be? Let's evaluate it. The line x := 15 declares a new variable within the scope of the if block. So, whenever x is modified inside that block, the changes apply only to the inner x, not to the oute…
